From 7345928cbf8f0bbcc14c38165a4800c0339d8717 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: scratch <scratch@local>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 20:03:11 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] arm64: dts: qcom: sc8280xp: add CPU cooling maps at 75 C

Every cpuN-thermal zone in sc8280xp.dtsi has a single 110 degC critical
trip and nothing else, so the CPUs run flat out until the thermal core
hits that trip and the kernel performs an emergency shutdown. There is
no gradual throttling anywhere in between. That is reachable on a
passively cooled machine: on a Huawei MateBook E Go (fanless 12.35"
tablet, no vents) a long gaming session has nothing standing between it
and an abrupt power-off.

Add a passive trip at 75 degC to each of the eight per-core zones and
bind it to the cpufreq cooling device of that core's own cluster.
All eight CPU nodes already declare #cooling-cells = <2>, so nothing
else is needed. 75 C matches the cooling policy used by the gaokun
Android kernel instead of the 85 C gpu-thermal value this started from.

Each map lists all four CPUs of the cluster, which is the idiom sm8250
and friends use: cpufreq cooling registers one device per policy, and
the thermal core picks whichever of the listed nodes owns it.

polling-delay-passive = <250> mirrors gpu-thermal so step_wise can
react. The 0-3 / 4-7 cluster split is confirmed on hardware: policy0
tops out at 2.44 GHz and policy4 at 2.69 GHz.

Based-on-patch-by: gaokun-android contributors <nekovahiru@gmail.com>
Signed-off-by: gaokun-android contributors <nekovahiru@gmail.com>

